## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of sliding-window quantile estimation with bounded memory—a practical problem in latency monitoring where old samples must fade out over time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance using a circular buffer
- Fixed-bin histogram design and bucket mapping
- Error bounds derived from bucket width

## Bucket Mapping
The value range [0, M) is divided into S equal-width buckets. A value x maps to bucket floor(x * S / M), giving each bucket a width of M/S.

## Choosing S
The number of buckets S trades off memory (O(S)) and accuracy. With bucket width M/S, the P95 estimate has error at most M/(2S). For 1% error, choose S ≈ 50*M. In practice, S = 100-1000 works well for typical latency ranges.

## Error Guarantees vs Exact Computation
- **Approximate P95**: The estimated percentile falls within the bucket containing the true P95. Maximum error is half the bucket width: M/(2S).
- **Exact P95**: Requires maintaining all W samples in the window and sorting them, which is O(W log W) per query.
- **Trade-off**: This histogram approach gives O(1) updates and O(S) queries with bounded error, vs O(1) updates and O(W log W) queries for exact computation.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowP95 {
  constructor(M, W, S) {
    this.M = M;
    this.W = W;
    this.S = S;
    this.buffer = new Int32Array(W);
    this.hist = new Int32Array(S);
    this.head = 0;
    this.count = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const b = Math.floor((x * this.S) / this.M);
    this.hist[b]++;

    if (this.count === this.W) {
      const y = this.buffer[this.head];
      const by = Math.floor((y * this.S) / this.M);
      this.hist[by]--;
    } else {
      this.count++;
    }

    this.buffer[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
  }

  query() {
    const p95 = Math.floor(0.95 * this.count);
    let cum = 0;
    for (let b = 0; b < this.S; b++) {
      cum += this.hist[b];
      if (cum >= p95) return (b * this.M) / this.S;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-uniform distributions or dynamic M?
- How does the choice of S affect query latency in a high-throughput system?

## Why This Is Asked
Analyzes streaming-frequency estimation with bounded memory and batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy hitters; counter trimming; candidate set.
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs memory; exact counting as baseline.
- Batch processing implications on update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Misra-Gries skeleton
class MG {
  constructor(K){ this.K=K; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){ if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1); else if(this.map.size<this.K) this.map.set(x,1); else { for(let [k,c] of this.map){ this.map.set(k,c-1); } /* remove zeros */ for(let [k,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(k)<=0) this.map.delete(k); } }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of space-time trade-offs in streaming analysis. Introduces hashing-based approximation and how it affects accuracy, latency, and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Hash bucketing for compact counts
- Amortized batch updates
- Probability of collision vs. accuracy
- Space-time trade-offs vs. exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
class BucketCounter {
  constructor(D) {
    this.D = D;
    this.buckets = new Array(D).fill(0);
    this.max = 0;
  }
  updateBatch(batch, hashFn) {
    for (const c of batch) {
      const b = hashFn(c) % this.D;
      this.buckets[b] += 1;
      if (this.buckets[b] > this.max) this.max = this.buckets[b];
    }
  }
  query(t) { return this.max >= t; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you bound false positives and choose D for a target ε? 
- What changes if batches vary in size or if t scales over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

